7] [17592186493780 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f14395c6-36b4-5cb6-8085-7a9935a781a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493812 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493812 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493812 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493812 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "28ce3e2a-2275-50a0-b25b-b9f3436f743c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493915 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493915 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493915 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493915 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,3]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions that return a nonzero value at exactly one point in $[0,3].$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $N$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,3]$\n\nIV. $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,3]$\n\n*Hint: Different functions from $N$ might give a nonzero value at different points in $[0,3].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493915 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ec301c4-26d6-5f2a-9132-b21928f5ee67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493915 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f({a}_{1})\\ne 0$ and $g({a}_{2})\\ne 0,$ where ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are distinct points in $[0,3],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({a}_{1})+g({a}_{1}) & \\ne 0 \\\\ f({a}_{2})+g({a}_{2}) & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)+g(x)\\ne 0$ at two distinct points. So, $N$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(a)\\ne 0$ for some $a\\in [0,3],$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\n$$\n\n \nfor any $x\\in [0,3].$ So, $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494008 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494008 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61b43f65-40c4-5ee2-a729-b625837e3d66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186493915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494029 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494029 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "876842ab-2531-5a55-8715-683cc46f3d77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494029 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186329727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494030 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494030 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87d932f6-49ba-5c24-8d19-a6ad3580ecf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494030 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494067 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef5989bf-617a-54c7-bbc7-9e531c1f1c5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494067 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186360468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495500 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495500 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495500 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495500 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495500 #edb/ref 1045 "Subspaces of Abstr