lection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551220 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551220 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f070b514-e12a-594a-9f0a-12adec04946c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551227 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551227 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551227 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551227 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc1d8442-a2da-505e-aaa8-1ed74221f3da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551256 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551256 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551256 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5},$ where $a\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "31d0899c-0442-5601-b2c5-58d91ebde08a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551256 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{5}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{5},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & ={a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{a}_{2}{t}^{5} \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{5}$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =ka{t}^{5} \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551281 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551281 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107105" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551281 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551281 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $B=\\{a{t}^{3}+b{t}^{2}+ct\\,|\\,a,b,c\\in Q\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551281 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60c155e6-f24c-5a27-86a4-e2d2fb148ceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551281 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{3}+{b}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{1}t$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{3}+{b}_{2}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}t$ are two polynomials from $B,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{3}+{b}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{1}t)+({a}_{2}{t}^{3}+{b}_{2}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}t) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{3}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}){t}^{2}+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $B$ since ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},\\,{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}$ are in $Q.$ So, $B$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(t)={t}^{2}$ and $k=\\sqrt{2},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B,$ because $\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551288 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551288 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551288 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551288 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{4}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $4,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{4}+b{t}^{2},$ with $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551288 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c7a58a4-6c9f-528f-bd18-00fe71c43a41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551288 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{4}+{b}_{1}{t}^{2}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{4}+{b}_{2}{t}^{2}$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{4}+{b}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{4}+{b}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{4}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}){t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{4}+b{t}^{2}\\in A$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{4}+b{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{4}+(kb){t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
